`UI.tsx` is a small shared layout primitives file that exports three building-block components used across all the settings sections. Here's what each one does:

---

### `Setting`
The card wrapper used by every individual settings section. It renders a styled box (pill background, border, rounded corners, padding) and accepts:
- **`children`** — the descriptive content/controls inside the card
- **`primaryAction`** — an optional element (typically a button) rendered below the children with a 10px gap. If absent, no extra spacing is added.

Every section in the settings page (`ThemeSettings`, `FormatSettings`, `EncryptionSettings`, etc.) wraps its content in this component to get the consistent card appearance.

---

### `AdvancedToggle`
Controls the show/hide behaviour of the Advanced Settings section. It has two states:

- **Collapsed** — renders a "Show advanced settings" text link.
- **Expanded** — renders an `id="advanced"` container with an "Advanced Settings" heading and all the child sections inside it.

The initial state is driven by `useLocation()` — if the URL hash is `#advanced` when the page loads, it opens automatically and scrolls itself into view. Clicking the link expands it (but there's no collapse button once open).

---

### `Column`
A layout helper for arranging related controls side by side within a `Setting` card. It renders a vertical stack with a bold title at the top and its children below, and is designed to sit alongside other `Column`s in a row. It accepts:
- **`title`** — a label string rendered in medium weight above the content
- **`children`** — the controls beneath the title
- **`style`** — optional style overrides

---

In summary, the file is purely structural — no business logic, no Redux, no API calls. It just provides the consistent visual scaffolding (`Setting` for cards, `AdvancedToggle` for the collapsible section, `Column` for side-by-side layouts within a card) that all the individual settings components build on top of.

